-- Original supplementary AEP study aids, version 2.
-- Prepared against the uploaded NCDC AEP Physics and Biology syllabi.
-- These are original explanatory notes, not reproduced NCDC pages.
-- Keep records in draft/review status until a subject reviewer checks every entry.
begin;

with revised_notes(id, summary, study_sections) as (
values
  ('da42bc93-ee15-4f99-8122-fccccd682dd2'::uuid, 'Measurement gives a numerical value and a unit for a physical quantity. This study aid follows the AEP Physics outcomes on choosing instruments, using SI units, recording accurate readings and applying density to practical situations.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"By the end of this topic, you should be able to identify physical quantities and units, select suitable measuring instruments, convert common units, record values sensibly and calculate density."},{"heading":"Quantities, units and instruments","content":"A physical measurement is expressed as a number and a unit. Common base quantities include length (metre, m), mass (kilogram, kg), time (second, s), electric current (ampere, A), temperature (kelvin, K) and amount of substance (mole, mol). Choose an instrument whose range and scale suit the measurement. Read scales at eye level where possible and record the unit with every result."},{"heading":"Accuracy, prefixes and density","content":"Prefixes help express multiples and fractions of units: kilo means 1,000; centi means one hundredth; milli means one thousandth. Repeat measurements when appropriate and report a reasonable number of significant figures. Density is mass divided by volume: density = mass ÷ volume. For a mass of 600 g and volume of 200 cm³, density = 3 g/cm³. Compare an object''s density with that of water to reason about floating or sinking."},{"heading":"Practical study","content":"Practise measuring length with a ruler or tape, time with a stopwatch, mass with a balance and volume by measuring or displacement. For irregular solids, record the initial and final water levels and use their difference as the object''s volume. Convert units before comparing results."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why must a measurement include a unit? 2. A block has mass 240 g and volume 80 cm³. Find its density. 3. Which method can determine the volume of an irregular stone? Answers: 1. The number alone does not state the scale used. 2. 3 g/cm³. 3. Water displacement."}]'::jsonb),
  ('5bf173dd-0c9e-43a6-a748-b15268a67712'::uuid, 'This topic connects forces, turning effects, energy, work, power, motion and pressure. The AEP syllabus emphasises describing effects, applying relationships, measuring and explaining everyday examples.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain what forces do; compare mass and weight; investigate turning effects; relate work, energy and power; describe motion; and calculate pressure in solids and fluids."},{"heading":"Forces and turning effects","content":"A force is a push or pull that can change motion or deform an object. Forces may involve contact, such as friction, or act at a distance, such as gravity and magnetic force. Mass measures the amount of matter in kilograms; weight is the gravitational force on a mass, W = mg. A force can turn an object about a pivot. The turning effect depends on the force and its perpendicular distance from the pivot."},{"heading":"Work, motion and pressure","content":"Work is done when a force moves an object through a distance in the force''s direction; work = force × distance. Power is the rate of doing work, power = work ÷ time. Describe motion using distance, time, speed and changes in velocity. Pressure is force per unit area, P = F/A, so the same force produces greater pressure over a smaller area. In fluids, pressure changes with depth."},{"heading":"Everyday applications","content":"Compare friction that is useful, such as grip when walking, with friction that wastes energy as heat. Explain why a long spanner gives a greater turning effect than a short one for the same force. Use labelled quantities and SI units when solving numerical problems."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. How does weight differ from mass? 2. What happens to pressure if the same force acts on half the area? 3. A 100 N force moves a load 3 m in its direction. Find the work done. Answers: 1. Weight is gravitational force and depends on gravitational field strength; mass is measured in kg. 2. Pressure doubles. 3. 300 J."}]'::jsonb),
  ('2e7f3a6b-4d45-4726-9a5f-f92f24a8ca20'::uuid, 'The AEP Light topic focuses on how light travels and behaves, including shadows, reflection and the formation of eclipses. Use diagrams and observations to explain what happens rather than memorising labels alone.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe rectilinear propagation, explain shadow and eclipse formation, and use simple ray diagrams to represent reflection and image formation."},{"heading":"How light travels","content":"In a uniform transparent medium, light travels in straight lines. When an opaque object blocks light, a shadow forms. The darkest central region of a shadow is the umbra; a partial-shadow region is the penumbra. A pinhole or simple lamp-and-object demonstration can show that light travels along straight paths."},{"heading":"Reflection and images","content":"Reflection occurs when light strikes a surface and returns to the original medium. For a plane reflecting surface, the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, measured from the normal. A ray diagram should show the incident ray, normal, reflected ray and angles clearly. Smooth surfaces produce orderly reflection; rough surfaces scatter reflected light in many directions."},{"heading":"Sun, Earth and Moon alignment","content":"A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon lies between the Sun and Earth and blocks some sunlight from reaching Earth. A lunar eclipse occurs when Earth lies between the Sun and Moon, so Earth''s shadow falls on the Moon. Eclipses depend on alignment; they do not happen every month because the Moon''s orbit is tilted relative to Earth''s orbit."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. State the law of reflection. 2. Distinguish umbra from penumbra. 3. Which body is between the Sun and Earth during a solar eclipse? Answers: 1. Angle of incidence equals angle of reflection. 2. Umbra is full shadow; penumbra is partial shadow. 3. The Moon."}]'::jsonb),
  ('72bb543e-1aca-4b1a-a8e9-a88de06bac80'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus groups general wave properties and sound. Learners are expected to describe wave motion, compare transverse and longitudinal waves, use wave quantities and explain how sound is produced and transmitted.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify the main parts and quantities of a wave, compare transverse and longitudinal motion, apply wave speed relationships and explain sound transmission."},{"heading":"Wave properties","content":"A wave transfers energy from one place to another without requiring the whole medium to travel with it. Amplitude measures the maximum displacement from equilibrium; wavelength is the distance between corresponding points on neighbouring waves; frequency is the number of cycles per second; period is the time for one cycle. Frequency is measured in hertz (Hz)."},{"heading":"Types and calculations","content":"In a transverse wave, oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of travel. In a longitudinal wave, oscillations are parallel to the direction of travel, producing compressions and rarefactions. Wave speed is related to frequency and wavelength by v = fλ. Check units before substituting values, for example hertz multiplied by metres gives metres per second."},{"heading":"Sound in everyday life","content":"Sound is produced by vibrating objects and travels through a material medium as a longitudinal wave. It cannot travel through a vacuum. Pitch is associated with frequency; loudness is associated with amplitude. Reflected sound may be heard as an echo when the delay is sufficient. Compare how sound travels through solids, liquids and gases using careful observations."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What does wavelength measure? 2. A wave has frequency 50 Hz and wavelength 0.18 m. Calculate its speed. 3. Can sound travel through a vacuum? Answers: 1. Distance between corresponding points on successive waves. 2. 9 m/s. 3. No, sound requires a medium."}]'::jsonb),
  ('8b3f1c80-6523-4a79-9525-a095209247ac'::uuid, 'This AEP topic covers the Sun–Earth–Moon system, the Solar System, stars and galaxies. The notes below organise these ideas into scale, motion and observable effects.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe the relative movements of the Sun, Earth and Moon; identify major Solar System components; and explain basic ideas about stars and galaxies."},{"heading":"Sun, Earth and Moon","content":"Earth rotates on its axis, producing the cycle of day and night. Earth orbits the Sun, while the Moon orbits Earth. The changing relative positions of these bodies explain lunar phases and, when alignment is suitable, eclipses. Use labelled diagrams and arrows to distinguish rotation from revolution."},{"heading":"The Solar System","content":"The Solar System consists of the Sun and objects gravitationally bound to it, including planets, their moons, dwarf planets, asteroids and comets. Planets orbit the Sun; their distances and orbital periods differ. The Sun is the main source of light and heat for Earth and provides energy that supports many Earth systems."},{"heading":"Stars and galaxies","content":"A star is a hot luminous object that produces energy through processes in its interior. Stars differ in brightness, colour, size and stage in their life cycles. A galaxy contains many stars along with gas, dust and other matter. Our Solar System is part of the Milky Way galaxy. Astronomy uses observations and evidence to investigate objects too distant to visit."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What causes day and night? 2. Name two types of objects besides planets found in the Solar System. 3. What is a galaxy? Answers: 1. Earth''s rotation. 2. Examples include moons, asteroids, comets or dwarf planets. 3. A large system of stars and other matter held together by gravity."}]'::jsonb),
  ('6fcca51a-eb9e-47e1-917a-3c61c99ee352'::uuid, 'Thermal Physics combines the particle model of matter, temperature, heat transfer, expansion and heat quantities. The AEP syllabus expects learners to connect observations with particle behaviour and apply relevant relationships.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Use a particle model to describe matter, distinguish heat from temperature, explain heat transfer and expansion, and solve basic heat-quantity problems."},{"heading":"Particle model and temperature","content":"Matter is made of particles in constant motion. In a solid, particles vibrate around fixed positions; in liquids they move past one another; in gases they move freely and are far apart. Temperature indicates how hot or cold a body is and is related to average particle kinetic energy. Heat is energy transferred because of a temperature difference, not a substance stored inside an object."},{"heading":"Transfer and expansion","content":"Conduction transfers energy through matter without bulk movement, convection transfers energy through moving fluids, and radiation transfers energy by electromagnetic waves and can occur through a vacuum. Heating usually increases particle motion and can cause expansion; cooling usually causes contraction. Explain examples such as gaps in bridges, liquid thermometers and ventilation using the relevant mechanism."},{"heading":"Heat quantities and calculations","content":"For a temperature change without a change of state, heat energy may be calculated using Q = mcΔT, where m is mass, c is specific heat capacity and ΔT is temperature change. Use consistent units and state assumptions. During a change of state, energy is used to change the arrangement of particles rather than simply raise temperature."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Name the three main methods of heat transfer. 2. Why can radiation travel through space? 3. What does each symbol in Q = mcΔT represent? Answers: 1. Conduction, convection and radiation. 2. It does not require a material medium. 3. Q is energy transferred, m mass, c specific heat capacity and ΔT temperature change."}]'::jsonb),
  ('11bd430f-b454-426d-8ff0-8cd9276514fb'::uuid, 'The syllabus brings together static electricity, current electricity, practical electrical safety, magnets, electromagnets and electromagnetic induction. These ideas are linked through charge, fields and energy transfer.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe charging and electric current, interpret simple circuits, use basic electrical quantities, explain magnetic effects and outline electromagnetic induction."},{"heading":"Charge and current","content":"Static electricity concerns electric charge that accumulates on objects. Like charges repel and unlike charges attract. Current electricity is the movement of charge through a circuit. Current is measured in amperes (A), potential difference in volts (V), and resistance in ohms (Ω). For a suitable ohmic conductor at constant physical conditions, V = IR."},{"heading":"Circuits and safety","content":"A circuit needs a complete conducting path and an energy source. Draw standard circuit symbols and identify series and parallel arrangements. Practical electricity involves insulation, correct fuses or circuit breakers, earthing where appropriate, and avoiding contact with live conductors. Never experiment with mains electricity; use teacher-approved low-voltage equipment."},{"heading":"Magnets and induction","content":"Magnets have north and south poles; like poles repel and unlike poles attract. A current-carrying coil produces a magnetic field and can form an electromagnet, whose strength depends on factors such as current, turns and the core. Electromagnetic induction occurs when a changing magnetic field through a conductor induces a voltage. This principle underlies generators and transformers."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. State the relationship between voltage, current and resistance for an ohmic conductor. 2. How can an electromagnet be made stronger? 3. What change produces electromagnetic induction? Answers: 1. V = IR. 2. Increase current or coil turns, within safe limits, and use a suitable core. 3. A changing magnetic field through the conductor or coil."}]'::jsonb),
  ('128a52e2-e239-49ff-9c02-89bfb3aa9943'::uuid, 'This topic introduces atomic structure, cathode rays and X-rays, and radioactivity. The focus is on models, evidence, applications and safe handling of information about radiation.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe a simple atomic model, distinguish the roles of subatomic particles, outline cathode-ray and X-ray ideas, and explain radioactivity and basic precautions."},{"heading":"Atomic structure","content":"An atom contains a small nucleus with positively charged protons and neutral neutrons, surrounded by negatively charged electrons. The number of protons identifies the element; atoms with different neutron numbers are isotopes of the same element. Models are used to explain observations and may be refined as evidence improves."},{"heading":"Cathode rays and X-rays","content":"Cathode rays are streams of electrons observed in discharge tubes under suitable conditions. X-rays are high-energy electromagnetic radiation that can penetrate matter to different extents. Their uses include medical imaging and industrial inspection, but exposure must be controlled because ionising radiation can damage living tissue."},{"heading":"Radioactivity and safety","content":"Radioactivity is the spontaneous emission of radiation from unstable atomic nuclei. Different types of radiation have different penetrating and ionising properties. Applications include medical diagnosis and treatment, dating materials and industrial measurement. Safe practice relies on minimising exposure time, maximising distance and using appropriate shielding and trained supervision."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Which particle determines an element''s identity? 2. How do X-rays differ from cathode rays? 3. Give two basic ways to reduce radiation exposure. Answers: 1. The proton. 2. X-rays are electromagnetic radiation; cathode rays are electrons. 3. Reduce time, increase distance, and use suitable shielding."}]'::jsonb),
  ('fc980975-ed68-4e80-ba67-bfcf4c54f566'::uuid, 'This topic examines satellites and how they support communication. Study the link between orbit, signal transmission and practical applications, while recognising that real systems also depend on ground equipment and coverage.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain what an artificial satellite is, describe common uses and outline how satellites support communication between distant locations."},{"heading":"Orbits and uses","content":"An artificial satellite is an object placed in orbit around Earth or another body. Gravity provides the centripetal acceleration needed to keep it in orbit. Different orbits suit different purposes: some satellites observe Earth, some support navigation, and some relay communication signals. A satellite does not remain above the same point for every orbit; geostationary satellites have a special orbit and period that make them appear fixed relative to Earth''s surface."},{"heading":"Communication link","content":"A typical satellite communication system includes a transmitting ground station, an uplink, the satellite payload that receives and retransmits or processes the signal, a downlink and a receiving station. Satellite systems can serve remote areas and wide regions, but performance may be affected by line of sight, weather at some frequencies, latency, cost and equipment requirements."},{"heading":"Applications and evaluation","content":"Examples include television distribution, telephone and data links, navigation and weather monitoring. Compare satellite links with terrestrial networks in terms of coverage, infrastructure and delay. Distinguish communication satellites from satellites used mainly for Earth observation or scientific research."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What role does gravity play in an orbit? 2. What are uplink and downlink? 3. Name one advantage and one limitation of satellite communication. Answers: 1. It provides the acceleration that bends the satellite''s path into an orbit. 2. Uplink sends a signal to the satellite; downlink sends it back toward a receiving station. 3. Wide coverage is an advantage; cost or signal delay can be a limitation."}]'::jsonb),
  ('99c7bae7-dbb9-4390-9442-cb282f25e339'::uuid, 'Digital electronics represents information using discrete signal states, commonly interpreted as binary 0 and 1. The AEP topic is a starting point for understanding digital logic and its use in everyday devices.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain binary states, recognise the purpose of basic logic gates and interpret simple truth tables and digital-system examples."},{"heading":"Binary signals","content":"A digital signal represents information using distinct levels rather than a continuously varying range. Binary systems use two states, commonly labelled 0 and 1. These states can represent off/on or false/true, depending on the circuit. A binary number uses powers of two, so each position has a place value such as 1, 2, 4, 8 and so on."},{"heading":"Logic gates","content":"Logic gates carry out simple logical operations on input signals. An AND gate gives an output of 1 only when all inputs are 1. An OR gate gives an output of 1 when at least one input is 1. A NOT gate reverses its input. A truth table lists all possible input combinations and the output, helping verify the behaviour of a circuit."},{"heading":"Applications","content":"Digital logic is used in calculators, computers, control systems, alarms and communication devices. Complex systems combine many simple operations. When analysing a circuit, identify inputs and outputs first, write the truth table and check each row against the gate rule."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. How many states does a binary digit represent? 2. When does an AND gate output 1? 3. What does a NOT gate do? Answers: 1. Two. 2. When every input is 1. 3. It reverses the input state."}]'::jsonb),
  ('66492504-c630-44eb-8848-8918836946bd'::uuid, 'The NCDC AEP Biology syllabus introduces Biology as the study of life and asks learners to relate it to observable life processes, practical work and laboratory safety.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain the meaning and importance of Biology, identify examples of living things and describe why careful observation and safe practical work matter."},{"heading":"Biology and living organisms","content":"Biology is the study of living organisms and their interactions. Organisms show life processes such as nutrition, respiration, growth, excretion, sensitivity and reproduction, although not every individual displays every process at every moment. Evidence should come from observation and appropriate investigation rather than appearance alone."},{"heading":"Biology in everyday life","content":"Biology helps people understand health, food production, conservation, disease, ecosystems and the use of living resources. Scientific explanations are strengthened by observations, records, comparisons and fair tests. Use biological terms carefully and distinguish a direct observation from an explanation inferred from it."},{"heading":"Practical and safety skills","content":"When using a laboratory or field setting, follow instructions, handle specimens and equipment responsibly, avoid tasting or directly smelling unknown substances, and report accidents. Record observations clearly, include labels on diagrams and respect living organisms and the environment."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What does Biology study? 2. Name three life processes. 3. Why should an observation be recorded carefully? Answers: 1. Living organisms and their interactions. 2. Examples include nutrition, respiration, growth, excretion, sensitivity or reproduction. 3. To preserve evidence and allow others to understand or check it."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a63652a2-80b7-47a3-9bac-cdea053dcf88'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus presents the cell as the basic unit of living organisms. Learners should identify cell structures, relate structure to function and compare plant and animal cells using labelled diagrams.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain why the cell is a basic unit of life, identify common cell structures and compare typical plant and animal cells."},{"heading":"Cell organisation","content":"Living organisms are made of one or more cells. Cells carry out essential life processes, and specialised cells have structures suited to their roles. In multicellular organisms, cells may form tissues, tissues form organs, and organs work together in systems."},{"heading":"Structures and functions","content":"The cell membrane controls movement of substances into and out of the cell. Cytoplasm is where many chemical reactions occur. The nucleus contains genetic material and helps control cell activities. Plant cells typically also have a cellulose cell wall, a large permanent vacuole and chloroplasts in photosynthetic tissues. Animal cells do not have a cell wall or chloroplasts."},{"heading":"Microscopy and diagrams","content":"A clear biological drawing has a title, neat single lines, labels that do not cross, and appropriate proportions. If observing cells through a microscope, begin with a low-power objective, focus carefully and use a prepared slide as instructed. Do not claim a structure is visible unless the preparation and magnification support that observation."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why is a cell called the basic unit of life? 2. Name two structures commonly found in plant cells but not animal cells. 3. What is the role of the cell membrane? Answers: 1. Cells are the smallest units that perform the processes of life. 2. Cell wall and chloroplasts are examples; a large permanent vacuole is another. 3. It regulates exchange with the surroundings."}]'::jsonb),
  ('755183e9-ffcd-4b95-be08-82e23e0097df'::uuid, 'Classification organises living organisms into groups based on shared characteristics. The AEP syllabus asks learners to understand levels of classification and recognise the five kingdoms through examples and identification activities.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain why classification is useful, recognise major taxonomic ranks and use observable characteristics to identify organisms."},{"heading":"Why classify?","content":"There are many different organisms. Classification provides a systematic way to name, compare and study them, and helps scientists communicate consistently. Organisms are grouped using shared characteristics and evidence about how they are related. Similar appearance alone may not always show close relationship."},{"heading":"Levels and kingdoms","content":"A common hierarchy is kingdom, phylum (division in plants), class, order, family, genus and species. Each lower rank generally groups organisms with more characteristics in common. The syllabus introduces five kingdoms: Monera, Protoctista, Fungi, Plantae and Animalia. Examples should be placed according to the features taught in the course, and classification systems may be updated as scientific evidence develops."},{"heading":"Identification skills","content":"An identification key guides a user through choices based on observable features. Choose features that are clear and mutually distinguishable, follow the key in order, and record the evidence supporting the identification. Do not guess a species name when the key does not provide enough information."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Give one reason for classifying organisms. 2. Put genus and species in the correct order from broader to narrower. 3. What does an identification key use? Answers: 1. It organises diversity and supports reliable communication. 2. Genus, then species. 3. A sequence of choices based on observable characteristics."}]'::jsonb),
  ('826c9cb9-d617-48db-883c-97a19e083568'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus focuses on insect characteristics, their diversity and their effects or roles in the environment. Learners should observe structure and connect features with function.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Recognise common insect features, describe how body structures support function and explain examples of beneficial and harmful effects."},{"heading":"Recognising insects","content":"Insects are arthropods with a body divided into head, thorax and abdomen, three pairs of jointed legs and one pair of antennae. Many adult insects have wings, but not all do. These features help distinguish insects from spiders, which have four pairs of legs and two main body regions."},{"heading":"Structure, function and life cycles","content":"Antennae are sensory structures; legs support movement and may be adapted for jumping, swimming or grasping; mouthparts differ according to feeding method. Insects may develop through incomplete metamorphosis, with young stages resembling smaller adults, or complete metamorphosis, with egg, larva, pupa and adult stages."},{"heading":"Roles in human life and ecosystems","content":"Some insects pollinate crops, decompose organic material or provide food for other organisms. Others damage crops, stored products or transmit disease-causing organisms. Explain the effect of a particular insect using evidence about its feeding, habitat or life cycle rather than labelling all insects as harmful."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Name the three main body regions of an insect. 2. How many pairs of legs do insects have? 3. Give one useful and one harmful effect of insects. Answers: 1. Head, thorax and abdomen. 2. Three pairs. 3. Examples: pollination is useful; crop damage or disease transmission can be harmful."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e206a5fc-2d45-46e8-8a27-aadef7136c01'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus explores the structures of flowering plants and how each part supports functions such as nutrition, transport, support and reproduction.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify major parts of a flowering plant, relate structures to their functions and describe how flowers contribute to reproduction."},{"heading":"Vegetative parts","content":"Roots anchor a plant and absorb water and mineral ions; root systems may also store food. Stems support leaves and flowers and contain transport tissues. Leaves provide a large surface for light capture and gas exchange and are major sites of photosynthesis. The arrangement and shape of plant parts vary with species and habitat."},{"heading":"Flower structure and reproduction","content":"A typical flower may contain sepals, petals, stamens and carpels. Stamens produce pollen; the carpel includes stigma, style and ovary, which contains ovules. Pollination is transfer of pollen to a receptive stigma. Fertilisation occurs when male and female gamete nuclei fuse. After successful fertilisation, ovules develop into seeds and the ovary often develops into a fruit."},{"heading":"Observation and drawing","content":"Examine a locally available flowering plant without damaging it unnecessarily. Draw and label structures accurately, and distinguish what you observed from what you infer about function. Compare flower features that may support insect or wind pollination only where the evidence is visible."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Give two functions of roots. 2. Which flower part produces pollen? 3. What is pollination? Answers: 1. Anchorage and absorption of water/mineral ions; some roots store food. 2. The anther, part of the stamen. 3. Transfer of pollen to a receptive stigma."}]'::jsonb),
  ('140dc61a-5ea0-4ac1-b6b1-d5c4acdc1f26'::uuid, 'This topic examines soil components and properties and how these affect plant growth. The AEP syllabus encourages learners to compare soil samples through observation and simple tests.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe common soil components, distinguish physical from chemical properties and relate soil properties to water movement and plant growth."},{"heading":"Soil components and physical properties","content":"Soil commonly contains mineral particles, organic matter, water, air and living organisms. Texture describes the relative proportions of sand, silt and clay. Sandy soil usually drains quickly; clay-rich soil holds more water but may drain slowly; loam has a mixture of particle sizes and can support many crops when well managed. Structure, porosity and water-holding capacity also influence root growth."},{"heading":"Chemical properties and fertility","content":"Soil pH indicates acidity or alkalinity and affects nutrient availability and the organisms living in soil. Nutrients such as nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium are needed for plant growth, but excessive or insufficient nutrients can cause problems. Soil fertility depends on several interacting factors, not colour alone."},{"heading":"Simple investigations","content":"Compare samples by feel, sedimentation in water, drainage or pH tests where suitable equipment and safe procedures are available. Keep the method consistent across samples and record results in a table. Do not identify a soil type from one observation alone; combine evidence from texture, drainage and other measured properties."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Name four components of soil. 2. Why can clay-rich soil become waterlogged? 3. What does soil pH describe? Answers: 1. Mineral particles, organic matter, water, air and living organisms (any four). 2. Fine particles and small pores can slow drainage. 3. Its acidity or alkalinity."}]'::jsonb),
  ('6fd87861-882e-4c57-a4a6-d4d4720df729'::uuid, 'The syllabus asks learners to explain why soil fertility should be protected and to relate erosion causes and effects to practical conservation methods.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify causes and effects of soil erosion, explain its effect on soil fertility and compare practical ways to reduce soil loss."},{"heading":"Causes and effects","content":"Soil erosion is the removal of topsoil by agents such as running water, wind or human activity. Bare ground, steep slopes, intense rainfall, overgrazing and poor cultivation practices can increase erosion. Loss of topsoil removes organic matter and nutrients, reduces crop productivity and may deposit sediment in rivers or drainage channels."},{"heading":"Conservation methods","content":"Maintaining plant cover protects soil from raindrop impact and slows runoff. Contour farming, terraces, mulching, cover crops, windbreaks and controlled grazing can reduce soil movement when suited to the site. Planting trees and managing drainage can help, but methods must fit local slope, rainfall, soil and land use."},{"heading":"Apply to a local area","content":"Observe a safe site and identify signs such as rills, exposed roots, deposited sediment or bare patches. Link each observation to a likely cause and propose a realistic intervention. Explain how the proposed method reduces erosion rather than simply listing its name."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why is topsoil valuable? 2. Name two ways to reduce water erosion. 3. Why should conservation methods suit local conditions? Answers: 1. It contains much of the soil''s organic matter and nutrients. 2. Examples include maintaining vegetation, contour farming, mulching or terracing. 3. Rainfall, slope, soil and land use differ."}]'::jsonb),
  ('219098ac-fdcb-4e57-ba7a-e3a8b4db52fa'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus introduces nutrition, food nutrients, their sources and importance, and practical food tests. The notes support the outcomes without replacing the syllabus activities.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Define nutrition, distinguish broad nutrition types, identify major nutrient groups and describe the purpose and limits of simple food tests."},{"heading":"Nutrition types","content":"Nutrition is the process by which organisms obtain and use materials for energy, growth, repair and regulation. Autotrophs make organic food from simpler substances, commonly using light energy in photosynthesis. Heterotrophs obtain organic nutrients from other organisms or their products. These terms describe how organisms obtain food, not whether they are healthy."},{"heading":"Nutrient groups","content":"Carbohydrates are an important energy source; lipids store energy and contribute to membranes; proteins support growth, repair and many cell functions; vitamins and minerals are needed in smaller quantities for normal body processes; water supports transport and chemical reactions; fibre supports healthy movement through the digestive tract. Different foods contain different mixtures of nutrients."},{"heading":"Food tests and evidence","content":"School food tests may use iodine solution for starch, Benedict''s reagent with heating for reducing sugars, Biuret reagent for protein and an ethanol emulsion test for lipids. Follow the teacher''s safety instructions, use small samples and controls where possible, and record the observed colour change. A test result supports a conclusion about the tested sample; it does not by itself prove that a food is nutritionally complete."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Distinguish autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition. 2. Which common test detects starch? 3. Why is a balanced diet more than one nutrient? Answers: 1. Autotrophs make organic food from simpler materials; heterotrophs obtain it from other organisms or products. 2. Iodine solution. 3. The body needs a range of nutrients and water for different functions."}]'::jsonb),
  ('dc2d1fb2-e93c-4cd9-9106-6460c2906515'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus emphasises that green plants are autotrophic and make food through photosynthesis. Learners should relate leaf structure, requirements and experimental evidence to this process.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain photosynthesis, identify the raw materials and conditions needed, relate leaf structures to function and interpret simple investigations."},{"heading":"Photosynthesis","content":"Photosynthesis uses light energy absorbed by chlorophyll to make carbohydrate from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen. A simplified word equation is carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, in the presence of light and chlorophyll. Glucose may be used in respiration, converted to other substances or stored as starch."},{"heading":"Leaf adaptations and requirements","content":"Leaves generally provide a broad surface for light capture. Chloroplasts contain chlorophyll; stomata allow gas exchange; veins transport water and dissolved substances and carry sugars away. Photosynthesis depends on factors including light intensity, carbon dioxide availability, temperature and water supply. A shortage of one limiting factor can reduce the rate even if other factors are plentiful."},{"heading":"Investigating starch","content":"A classic starch test involves destarching a plant, exposing part of a leaf to light under controlled conditions, then testing the leaf for starch using the teacher''s approved method. Alcohol used to remove chlorophyll is flammable and must be heated safely in a water bath under supervision, never over a naked flame. Control variables and explain what the result supports."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. State the word equation for photosynthesis. 2. What is chlorophyll''s role? 3. Why is a control important in an experiment? Answers: 1. Carbon dioxide + water → glucose + oxygen, with light and chlorophyll. 2. It absorbs light energy. 3. It helps show whether the changed factor explains the result."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a1a68619-a008-4f84-9e98-7017fac01f60'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus presents mammals as heterotrophs that obtain nutrients by feeding and digesting food. Learners should connect the digestive system''s structures to digestion, absorption and use of nutrients.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain heterotrophic nutrition, outline the main stages of nutrition in mammals and relate digestive organs to their functions."},{"heading":"Stages of nutrition","content":"The main stages are ingestion, digestion, absorption, assimilation and egestion. Digestion breaks large insoluble food molecules into smaller soluble molecules that can be absorbed. Enzymes speed up specific chemical reactions; they work best within suitable conditions and are not used up by the reaction."},{"heading":"Digestive system","content":"The mouth takes in and mechanically breaks food; saliva begins starch digestion. The stomach mixes food with acid and enzymes, including those involved in protein digestion. The small intestine receives digestive secretions and is the main site of digestion completion and nutrient absorption. Villi and microvilli increase surface area. The large intestine absorbs water and forms faeces. The liver produces bile, which helps emulsify fats; the pancreas releases digestive enzymes and other secretions."},{"heading":"Diet and health","content":"A balanced diet supplies suitable amounts of energy, protein, fats, vitamins, minerals, fibre and water. Requirements vary with age, activity and health. Explain the importance of chewing, hygiene and safe food handling, and avoid claiming that one food alone provides every nutrient needed."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Distinguish digestion from absorption. 2. How do villi help the small intestine? 3. What is the role of bile in fat digestion? Answers: 1. Digestion breaks food molecules down; absorption moves soluble products into blood or lymph. 2. They increase absorptive surface area. 3. It emulsifies fat into smaller droplets, aiding enzyme action."}]'::jsonb),
  ('7c5a224c-8670-4e66-98ae-43cd748c204a'::uuid, 'This AEP topic examines how water, mineral ions and manufactured food move through plants. The notes focus on the role of specialised tissues and the factors affecting transport.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain how water and mineral ions enter plants, distinguish xylem from phloem and describe the role of transpiration in water movement."},{"heading":"Water and mineral uptake","content":"Water enters root hair cells by osmosis when water potential is higher in the soil solution than inside the cells. Mineral ions may be absorbed by active transport or other transport processes. Root hairs provide a large surface area for absorption. Water moves across root tissues toward the xylem and is transported to stems and leaves."},{"heading":"Xylem, phloem and transpiration","content":"Xylem transports water and dissolved mineral ions mainly upward and also helps support the plant. Phloem transports sugars and other organic substances from sources to sinks; movement can occur in different directions in different sieve tubes. Transpiration is water loss as vapour, mainly through stomata. Evaporation from leaf surfaces helps pull water through the xylem."},{"heading":"Factors and investigations","content":"Transpiration rate may be affected by light, temperature, humidity, air movement and leaf area. A potometer estimates water uptake, which can be used as an indicator of transpiration under controlled conditions; water uptake is not identical to water loss because some water is used by the plant. Change one factor at a time and keep other conditions as constant as possible."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What does xylem transport? 2. What does phloem transport? 3. Name two factors that can affect transpiration. Answers: 1. Water and dissolved mineral ions. 2. Sugars and other organic substances. 3. Examples include light, temperature, humidity, wind/air movement and leaf area."}]'::jsonb),
  ('bc7b3352-085c-4086-8dc5-5bbd44e54dd4'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus considers why animals need transport systems and how blood and circulation move materials between exchange surfaces and body tissues.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain the need for transport in multicellular animals, identify main components of blood and describe the basic route of circulation in mammals."},{"heading":"Why transport systems are needed","content":"In large multicellular organisms, many cells are far from the body surface, so diffusion alone is too slow to deliver all required substances and remove wastes. A transport system carries oxygen, nutrients, hormones and wastes between organs and tissues. Exchange still occurs across thin surfaces such as capillary walls."},{"heading":"Blood and the heart","content":"Blood contains plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells and platelets. Plasma carries dissolved substances; red blood cells contain haemoglobin to transport oxygen; white blood cells help defend against pathogens; platelets support clotting. The mammalian heart pumps blood through a double circulatory system: one circuit carries blood between heart and lungs, and the other between heart and the rest of the body."},{"heading":"Vessels and health","content":"Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins return blood to the heart, and capillaries have thin walls that support exchange. Vessel structure relates to pressure and function. Exercise, nutrition, smoking, inherited factors and some diseases can affect cardiovascular health. Explain the relationship between structure and function rather than relying only on memorised definitions."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why is a transport system useful in a large animal? 2. What is the main role of red blood cells? 3. Which vessels are the main sites of exchange? Answers: 1. Diffusion alone is too slow over long distances. 2. Transport oxygen using haemoglobin. 3. Capillaries."}]'::jsonb),
  ('dbdd21de-d42f-4d1e-9ed4-a629f0e6eb0f'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus asks learners to explain why multicellular organisms need specialised gaseous exchange surfaces and to identify adaptations of the human respiratory system.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain the need for gaseous exchange, identify features of an efficient exchange surface and trace air movement through the human respiratory system."},{"heading":"Exchange and diffusion","content":"Gaseous exchange is the movement of oxygen and carbon dioxide between an organism and its environment. Diffusion occurs down a concentration gradient. Large multicellular organisms need specialised surfaces because their surface-area-to-volume ratio is relatively small and cells are far from the outside environment."},{"heading":"Adaptations of exchange surfaces","content":"Efficient exchange surfaces tend to have a large surface area, a thin barrier, a moist surface and a good supply of blood or ventilation to maintain concentration gradients. In humans, air passes through the nose or mouth, trachea, bronchi and bronchioles to alveoli. Alveoli provide a large area, thin walls and close contact with capillaries."},{"heading":"Ventilation and health","content":"Ventilation moves air into and out of the lungs, maintaining gradients for diffusion. During inhalation, the diaphragm contracts and the thoracic cavity expands; during exhalation at rest, these muscles relax and the volume decreases. Smoke and harmful airborne particles can damage respiratory tissues and reduce gas-exchange efficiency."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why do multicellular organisms need specialised exchange surfaces? 2. Name two alveolar adaptations. 3. What happens to thoracic volume during inhalation? Answers: 1. Diffusion alone is insufficient over long distances. 2. Large surface area, thin moist walls and rich capillary supply are examples. 3. It increases."}]'::jsonb),
  ('66a6e2a1-ef67-4439-a1dc-010fa990b436'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus describes respiration as the process that releases energy from food for cellular activities and asks learners to compare aerobic and anaerobic pathways.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain why respiration is necessary, compare aerobic and anaerobic respiration and represent aerobic respiration with a word equation."},{"heading":"Aerobic respiration","content":"Aerobic respiration releases energy from glucose using oxygen. A simplified word equation is glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy. In eukaryotic cells, many stages occur in mitochondria, while the first stage occurs in the cytoplasm. The released energy supports movement, active transport, growth, repair and other cell processes."},{"heading":"Anaerobic respiration","content":"Anaerobic respiration releases energy without oxygen and transfers less energy from each glucose molecule than aerobic respiration. In human muscles, it can lead to lactate formation during intense activity. In yeast, anaerobic fermentation produces ethanol and carbon dioxide. The products depend on the organism and pathway, so do not use one equation for every case."},{"heading":"Investigation and comparison","content":"Compare conditions and products carefully in a teacher-supervised investigation, for example using yeast and a suitable sugar solution. Control temperature, amount of substrate and time where possible. Gas production may indicate activity but should be interpreted with the experimental setup in mind. Respiration is not the same as breathing: breathing ventilates the lungs; respiration is a cellular chemical process."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. State the word equation for aerobic respiration. 2. Give one difference between aerobic and anaerobic respiration. 3. Why is respiration important? Answers: 1. Glucose + oxygen → carbon dioxide + water + energy. 2. Aerobic uses oxygen and releases more energy per glucose; anaerobic does not use oxygen and releases less. 3. It provides energy for cellular activities."}]'::jsonb),
  ('dc09e118-3db9-438e-9679-3f771a972103'::uuid, 'This AEP topic examines how animals remove metabolic waste and how different organs contribute to excretion. It is important to distinguish excretion from egestion.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Define excretion, distinguish it from egestion and identify major waste products and organs involved in their removal in mammals."},{"heading":"Excretion versus egestion","content":"Excretion removes metabolic waste made by cells, such as carbon dioxide, excess water, salts and nitrogenous waste. Egestion removes undigested material from the digestive tract. They are different processes and should not be used interchangeably."},{"heading":"Organs and waste products","content":"The lungs remove carbon dioxide and water vapour. The kidneys filter blood, regulate water and ion balance, and remove nitrogenous waste in urine. The liver processes substances and converts toxic ammonia to urea in mammals; the skin can remove small amounts of water and salts in sweat. Each organ has other functions as well."},{"heading":"Kidneys and homeostasis","content":"The kidneys contain nephrons, where filtration and selective reabsorption help regulate the composition of blood. Useful substances such as glucose are normally reabsorbed under healthy conditions, while the final urine contains waste and variable amounts of water and salts. Hydration, disease and some medicines can affect kidney function; medical concerns require professional advice."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. How does excretion differ from egestion? 2. Which organs remove carbon dioxide? 3. What is one role of the kidneys? Answers: 1. Excretion removes metabolic waste; egestion removes undigested food. 2. The lungs. 3. Filter blood and regulate water, salts and nitrogenous waste."}]'::jsonb),
  ('befa85c9-857a-425f-a8ac-1047273e380c'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus describes chemical coordination through hormones and the organs that produce them. Learners should relate endocrine structures to their effects and recognise feedback as a way of regulating internal conditions.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain the role of hormones, distinguish endocrine from exocrine glands and give examples of chemical coordination in humans."},{"heading":"Hormones and endocrine glands","content":"Hormones are chemical messengers released by endocrine glands into the blood. They act on target cells or organs that have appropriate receptors. Endocrine glands release hormones into the bloodstream, while exocrine glands release products through ducts. Hormones may influence growth, metabolism, reproduction, stress responses and the regulation of blood glucose."},{"heading":"Examples and feedback","content":"The pancreas releases insulin and glucagon, which help regulate blood glucose. The thyroid gland produces hormones that influence metabolic rate. The pituitary gland releases hormones that affect other glands and body functions. Negative feedback helps maintain a variable near a suitable range: a change triggers responses that counteract that change."},{"heading":"Coordination compared","content":"Nervous coordination uses electrical impulses along neurones and often acts quickly and briefly. Hormonal coordination generally uses blood-borne chemical signals and may act more slowly or for longer. The two systems work together; they are not mutually exclusive."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What is a hormone? 2. How does an endocrine gland differ from an exocrine gland? 3. What is the purpose of negative feedback? Answers: 1. A chemical messenger released by cells or glands. 2. Endocrine glands release hormones into blood; exocrine glands use ducts. 3. It helps counter a change and maintain stable internal conditions."}]'::jsonb),
  ('e94668bb-9ff2-46e4-9b19-25b81782b809'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus introduces nerve impulses and the nervous system''s role in coordinating responses. Study the pathway from stimulus to response and the role of the central and peripheral nervous systems.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Describe the basic organisation of the human nervous system, outline how a nerve impulse travels and explain a simple reflex arc."},{"heading":"Neurones and impulses","content":"A neurone is a specialised cell that transmits electrical signals. Sensory neurones carry information from receptors toward the central nervous system; relay neurones connect pathways within it; motor neurones carry commands to effectors such as muscles or glands. At a synapse, chemical neurotransmitters commonly carry the signal across the gap between cells."},{"heading":"Central and peripheral systems","content":"The brain and spinal cord form the central nervous system. Nerves outside them form the peripheral nervous system, linking receptors and effectors with the central system. The brain integrates information and coordinates many responses; the spinal cord also carries signals and coordinates some reflexes."},{"heading":"Reflex actions","content":"A reflex is a rapid, automatic response to a particular stimulus. A typical reflex arc involves a receptor, sensory neurone, relay pathway in the central nervous system, motor neurone and effector. Reflexes can protect the body, but not every automatic behaviour follows the same pathway. Practical demonstrations must be safe and must not expose learners to harmful stimuli."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What is the role of a sensory neurone? 2. Name the main parts of a simple reflex arc. 3. Why can a reflex be protective? Answers: 1. It carries signals from a receptor toward the central nervous system. 2. Receptor, sensory neurone, central relay, motor neurone and effector. 3. It can produce a rapid response before conscious consideration."}]'::jsonb),
  ('c92b2dd5-41cf-4738-9856-6edb27726a74'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus focuses on sensory organs, especially the eye and ear, and how their structures detect stimuli. Learners should relate the receptor to the stimulus and the signal sent to the nervous system.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify the main structures of the eye and ear, relate their functions to vision and hearing, and explain how receptors convert stimuli into nerve signals."},{"heading":"The eye and vision","content":"Light enters through the cornea and pupil; the iris adjusts pupil size. The lens focuses light onto the retina, which contains photoreceptor cells. Signals travel through the optic nerve to the brain, where visual information is interpreted. The ciliary muscles and suspensory ligaments change lens shape to focus on near or distant objects."},{"heading":"The ear and hearing","content":"The pinna collects sound waves and directs them along the auditory canal to the eardrum. Vibrations pass through the middle-ear ossicles to the inner ear. In the cochlea, sensory hair cells respond to vibrations and generate signals carried by the auditory nerve. The semicircular canals are involved in balance, not hearing alone."},{"heading":"Stimuli, receptors and care","content":"A receptor is sensitive to a particular kind of stimulus, such as light, sound, pressure, temperature or chemicals. The nervous system interprets incoming signals. Protect eyes and ears from injury and excessive exposure to loud sound. A diagram should label structures accurately and explain function, not simply list names."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Where are the photoreceptors found? 2. What is the role of the eardrum? 3. Which inner-ear structure is important for hearing? Answers: 1. In the retina. 2. It vibrates in response to sound waves. 3. The cochlea."}]'::jsonb),
  ('d8b8c09f-0ee4-4de1-8d0e-d16694b0bd9f'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus links the skeleton and muscles to movement. Learners should explain how joints, bones, muscles and connective structures work together to produce locomotion.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify key functions of the skeleton, describe types of joints and explain how antagonistic muscles move a limb."},{"heading":"Skeleton and joints","content":"The skeleton supports the body, protects organs, provides attachment points for muscles and helps movement. Bones meet at joints. A hinge joint permits movement mainly in one plane, while a ball-and-socket joint permits movement in several directions. Cartilage reduces friction at joint surfaces; ligaments connect bone to bone."},{"heading":"Muscles and movement","content":"Skeletal muscles pull when they contract; they do not actively push bones. Many movements use antagonistic pairs. At the elbow, the biceps contracts and the triceps relaxes to bend the arm; to straighten it, the triceps contracts and the biceps relaxes. Tendons connect muscles to bones and transmit the pulling force."},{"heading":"Locomotion and care","content":"Locomotion is movement of the whole organism from place to place, while movement can also occur in a body part. Muscles require energy from respiration. Warm-up, suitable activity and safe lifting can reduce some injuries, but joint or muscle pain should not be diagnosed from a classroom model alone."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Give two functions of the skeleton. 2. What do tendons connect? 3. Why are biceps and triceps described as an antagonistic pair? Answers: 1. Support, protection, movement, muscle attachment or blood-cell production. 2. Muscle to bone. 3. They produce opposite actions at the joint."}]'::jsonb),
  ('eada864f-da12-48c7-83c3-85056755e392'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus examines how organisms increase in size during their lives and how growth can be observed or measured. Distinguish growth from development.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Define growth, identify ways to measure it and compare patterns of growth in plants and animals."},{"heading":"Meaning and measurement","content":"Growth is a lasting increase in size, mass, dry mass or cell number. It can be measured using height, length, mass or another suitable indicator over time. Measurements should be repeated at consistent intervals and taken with suitable instruments. Temporary changes caused by water content or posture should not be confused with permanent growth."},{"heading":"Growth in plants and animals","content":"Plants may continue producing new tissues at meristems, especially at root and shoot tips and in some stems. Animal growth often follows a more limited pattern and may include periods of rapid growth. Different species and individuals grow at different rates because of inherited factors and environmental conditions."},{"heading":"Factors affecting growth","content":"Nutrients, water, light for photosynthetic plants, temperature, oxygen and health can affect growth. A fair investigation changes one factor while keeping others as constant as practical. A graph of size against time can show patterns, but correlation alone does not prove that a single factor caused the pattern."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What is meant by growth? 2. Why should measurements be taken at regular intervals? 3. Name two factors that affect growth. Answers: 1. A lasting increase in size, mass or cell number. 2. To compare change over time consistently. 3. Examples include nutrition, water, light, temperature, oxygen and health."}]'::jsonb),
  ('256d5a2e-39ed-423f-a518-2b63828471fb'::uuid, 'This AEP topic concerns changes in form and function as organisms mature. Development is broader than an increase in size and may include cell differentiation and the formation of specialised structures.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Distinguish growth from development and describe how cells and body structures change as plants and animals mature."},{"heading":"Growth and development","content":"Growth is a measurable increase in size, mass or cell number. Development includes changes in structure, function and capability over the life cycle. Cells may differentiate, becoming specialised for different roles. An organism can therefore develop in complexity even when its overall size changes only slightly."},{"heading":"Life cycles","content":"Plants develop from seeds or other reproductive structures through stages that may include germination, seedling growth, vegetative maturity, flowering, fruiting and seed formation. Animals may develop directly or pass through larval and metamorphic stages. The sequence and timing differ among species; use the life cycle of the example being studied."},{"heading":"Observation and comparison","content":"Compare stages using diagrams, photographs or specimens where appropriate. Record visible changes such as leaf formation, body proportions, emergence of reproductive structures or changes in behaviour. Avoid assuming that all organisms share the same stages or timetable."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. How does development differ from growth? 2. What is cell differentiation? 3. Name two stages in a flowering plant''s life cycle. Answers: 1. Development includes changes in form and function; growth is an increase in size, mass or cell number. 2. Cells become specialised for particular roles. 3. Examples include germination, seedling, flowering, fruiting or seed formation."}]'::jsonb),
  ('cb6f8a1a-be33-4c4e-a18e-4ba8f783508a'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus explains that some plant parts can develop into new plants. Vegetative reproduction uses structures such as stems, roots or leaves rather than the fusion of gametes.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Define vegetative reproduction, identify natural and human-assisted methods and compare advantages and limitations with sexual reproduction."},{"heading":"How vegetative reproduction works","content":"Asexual reproduction produces new individuals from one parent without fusion of male and female gametes. In vegetative reproduction, new plants grow from structures such as runners, rhizomes, bulbs, tubers, suckers or stem and leaf cuttings. The offspring are usually genetically very similar to the parent, except when mutations occur."},{"heading":"Methods and examples","content":"Examples include potato tubers, onion bulbs, strawberry runners and cuttings used for some ornamental or crop plants. Farmers and gardeners may use cuttings, layering, grafting or other propagation techniques. The method depends on the plant species and the desired traits; grafting joins plant tissues and is not identical to every form of vegetative propagation."},{"heading":"Advantages and limitations","content":"Vegetative reproduction can produce plants quickly and preserve desirable characteristics. However, offspring may share susceptibility to the same disease and there may be less genetic variation than in sexual reproduction. Use clean tools and healthy planting material to reduce the spread of plant pathogens."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Why is vegetative reproduction asexual? 2. Name two vegetative structures. 3. Give one advantage and one limitation. Answers: 1. It does not involve fusion of gametes. 2. Examples include tubers, bulbs, runners, rhizomes or cuttings. 3. Rapid, consistent propagation is an advantage; low genetic variation can be a limitation."}]'::jsonb),
  ('67a55d5b-9cf8-47b5-b608-c3b959511e11'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus treats the flower as a specialised reproductive structure. Learners should identify reproductive parts and explain pollination, fertilisation and seed formation.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Label the main parts of a flower, distinguish pollination from fertilisation and outline the development of seeds and fruits."},{"heading":"Flower parts","content":"The stamen is the male reproductive structure and includes anther and filament. The carpel or pistil includes stigma, style and ovary containing ovules. Petals may attract pollinators and sepals often protect the flower bud. Flower structure varies, so label the specimen actually observed."},{"heading":"Pollination and fertilisation","content":"Pollination is transfer of pollen from an anther to a receptive stigma. It may occur within the same flower or plant, or between different plants of the same species. After compatible pollen germinates, a pollen tube grows toward an ovule and enables male gametes to reach the female gamete. Fertilisation is fusion of gamete nuclei; it is not the same as pollination."},{"heading":"Seeds and fruit","content":"After fertilisation, the ovule commonly develops into a seed and the ovary into a fruit. Seeds contain an embryo and food reserves or structures that support early growth. Dispersal reduces competition with the parent plant and may help a species reach new habitats. Wind, water, animals and explosive mechanisms are examples of dispersal routes."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Distinguish pollination from fertilisation. 2. Which flower structure contains ovules? 3. What commonly develops from the ovary? Answers: 1. Pollination transfers pollen; fertilisation fuses gamete nuclei. 2. The ovary. 3. A fruit."}]'::jsonb),
  ('9d264605-4e39-417f-97f1-b4b60aba2be1'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus addresses the male and female reproductive systems, the menstrual cycle, fertilisation and human development. Present this material scientifically and respectfully, using accurate terminology.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify the main reproductive organs and their functions, describe the menstrual cycle at an introductory level and explain fertilisation and early development."},{"heading":"Reproductive systems","content":"In the male system, testes produce sperm and testosterone; sperm travel through ducts and mix with fluids from glands to form semen. In the female system, ovaries release ova and produce hormones; the oviducts are common sites of fertilisation; the uterus supports development; the cervix connects the uterus to the vagina. Structures have specific functions and should be labelled accurately."},{"heading":"Cycle and fertilisation","content":"The menstrual cycle involves changes in the ovaries and uterine lining regulated by hormones. Ovulation is release of an ovum from an ovary. If sperm fertilises an ovum, a zygote forms and begins dividing as it travels toward the uterus. Implantation occurs when the developing embryo attaches to the uterine lining. If pregnancy does not occur, the uterine lining is shed during menstruation."},{"heading":"Health and respectful study","content":"Human reproduction should be discussed with accurate language, respect for privacy and attention to health, consent and reliable medical information. Menstrual timing varies among individuals and cycles. Classroom diagrams describe typical structures and processes, not every individual''s experience."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Where are sperm produced? 2. What is ovulation? 3. How does fertilisation differ from implantation? Answers: 1. In the testes. 2. Release of an ovum from an ovary. 3. Fertilisation is fusion of gametes; implantation is attachment of the developing embryo to the uterine lining."}]'::jsonb),
  ('af854396-13df-490b-b6fa-d7ac803cd8d0'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus introduces the transmission of characteristics from parents to offspring. Learners should connect inherited information with variation while distinguishing inherited traits from traits acquired through experience.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Explain inheritance, define basic genetic terms and use simple examples to describe how characteristics may pass from parents to offspring."},{"heading":"Genes and chromosomes","content":"DNA carries hereditary information. A gene is a segment of DNA associated with a functional product or trait, and genes are located on chromosomes. In sexual reproduction, offspring receive genetic material from each parent. Different forms of a gene are called alleles. The combination of alleles contributes to an organism''s genotype, while observable characteristics form its phenotype, influenced by genes and environment."},{"heading":"Variation and simple inheritance","content":"Individuals of the same species differ. Some variation is inherited; other variation results from environment or a combination of both. In simple dominant-recessive models, a dominant allele can determine the phenotype in a heterozygote, but not all real traits follow this pattern. Punnett squares can organise possible allele combinations; they predict probabilities, not certainty for an individual child."},{"heading":"Use evidence carefully","content":"Avoid assuming a person''s complete genetic make-up from one visible feature. Family resemblance may reflect inherited factors but does not establish exact parentage or health status. For classroom examples, state assumptions clearly and use the inheritance model specified in the question."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What is an allele? 2. Distinguish genotype and phenotype. 3. Why does a Punnett square not guarantee an individual outcome? Answers: 1. An alternative form of a gene. 2. Genotype is genetic allele combination; phenotype is observable characteristics. 3. It gives probabilities among possible combinations, not certainty for one outcome."}]'::jsonb),
  ('87e54ab0-d2f8-4044-a20c-c32762dbedea'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus introduces ecological organisation and the relationships between organisms and their environment. Learners should distinguish organism, population, community and ecosystem.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Define key ecological terms, describe biotic and abiotic factors and explain how organisms interact with their surroundings."},{"heading":"Levels of organisation","content":"An organism is an individual living thing. A population is a group of organisms of the same species in an area. A community consists of populations of different species interacting in an area. An ecosystem includes the community and the non-living environment, with interactions between them. A habitat is the place where an organism lives; a niche describes its role and resource use."},{"heading":"Biotic and abiotic factors","content":"Biotic factors include food availability, competition, predators, parasites and mates. Abiotic factors include light, temperature, water, soil, pH and mineral availability. These factors affect distribution, growth and survival. An organism''s response depends on the combination of conditions rather than a single factor in isolation."},{"heading":"Field study","content":"A simple ecological survey can record organisms and environmental conditions in a defined area. Use consistent sampling methods, identify organisms as accurately as possible and avoid damaging the habitat. Distinguish evidence collected during a survey from explanations about why a species is present or absent."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. How does a community differ from an ecosystem? 2. Give two abiotic factors. 3. What is a habitat? Answers: 1. An ecosystem includes the community plus the non-living environment and interactions. 2. Examples include light, temperature, water, soil pH or minerals. 3. The place where an organism lives."}]'::jsonb),
  ('67f0bda5-b6bb-48c8-8caa-2e545e7f6374'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus focuses on feeding relationships and interdependence. Food chains and webs represent the movement of energy through organisms in an ecosystem.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify producers and consumers, construct food chains, interpret food webs and explain why energy transfer limits the length of many chains."},{"heading":"Food chains","content":"A food chain shows a feeding sequence. Producers, such as green plants, make organic food; primary consumers feed on producers; higher-level consumers feed on other consumers. Decomposers break down dead material and wastes, returning nutrients to the environment. Arrows point in the direction of energy transfer, from the organism being eaten to the organism that eats it."},{"heading":"Food webs and energy","content":"A food web links several food chains because many organisms eat more than one kind of food and may have several predators. Energy is lost between trophic levels through respiration, movement, heat and waste, so less energy is available at higher levels. Matter such as nutrients is recycled, while energy flows through the ecosystem and ultimately dissipates as heat."},{"heading":"Predicting change","content":"If a population declines or increases, other populations may be affected through feeding relationships and competition. The outcome depends on the whole web and environmental conditions. Use arrows carefully and support predictions with the relationships shown rather than assuming every change has only one effect."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What does an arrow in a food chain show? 2. Why are producers important? 3. Why is less energy available at higher trophic levels? Answers: 1. Direction of energy transfer. 2. They convert external energy into chemical energy stored in organic matter. 3. Organisms use and lose energy through life processes, so not all is passed on."}]'::jsonb),
  ('3e141b19-1bd1-4fd1-b6e4-9f75b0344e18'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus examines natural interactions between organisms. Study the benefit, cost or lack of clear effect on each organism in the particular association.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Recognise common ecological associations and explain their effects on the organisms involved using examples."},{"heading":"Types of association","content":"Mutualism benefits both organisms, such as a pollinator receiving food while a flowering plant receives pollen transfer. Commensalism benefits one organism without a clear effect on the other. Parasitism benefits a parasite while harming its host. Predation involves one organism killing and eating another. Competition occurs when organisms require the same limited resource."},{"heading":"Context matters","content":"An interaction''s effect depends on the species and conditions. A species may compete for food in one setting and use different resources in another. Parasites may live on or inside hosts; predators may regulate prey populations. Avoid assigning an association based on a name alone; explain the actual benefit or harm described."},{"heading":"Observation and evidence","content":"In a field study, record the organisms, the behaviour or physical relationship observed and the conditions. A single observation may suggest an interaction but may not prove its long-term effect. Use more than one example when comparing association types."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. What is mutualism? 2. Which association benefits a parasite and harms a host? 3. Why does competition occur? Answers: 1. An interaction in which both organisms benefit. 2. Parasitism. 3. Organisms need the same limited resource."}]'::jsonb),
  ('a9db6840-8325-4d3c-93b3-e122240cb5a5'::uuid, 'The AEP syllabus asks learners to appreciate Uganda''s natural environments and consider how human activities affect them. The focus is on evidence, consequences and sustainable responses.', '[{"heading":"Learning goals","content":"Identify examples of natural environments, explain how human activities affect ecosystems and evaluate practical ways to reduce environmental harm."},{"heading":"Ecosystems and human dependence","content":"People depend on natural systems for food, water, soil fertility, materials, energy and other services. Uganda contains varied environments, including forests, wetlands, grasslands, lakes, rivers and cultivated landscapes. Each supports particular organisms and provides benefits, but each can be affected by land use and resource demand."},{"heading":"Pressures and consequences","content":"Deforestation, wetland drainage, pollution, unsustainable harvesting, soil erosion, habitat fragmentation and greenhouse-gas emissions can alter ecosystems. Consequences may include loss of biodiversity, reduced water quality, lower soil productivity and increased risk from floods or droughts. Effects differ by location and should be supported with local observations or reliable evidence."},{"heading":"Conservation and sustainability","content":"Possible responses include protecting habitats, restoring degraded land, planting suitable native vegetation, managing waste, conserving water, using resources efficiently and involving local communities. A sustainable choice considers environmental, social and economic effects over time. Evaluate actions by asking who benefits, what trade-offs exist and how success can be measured."},{"heading":"Check your understanding","content":"1. Name two ecosystem services people depend on. 2. Give two possible consequences of wetland loss. 3. What makes an environmental action sustainable? Answers: 1. Examples include food, clean water, soil fertility and materials. 2. Examples include reduced water filtration, habitat loss or changed flood patterns. 3. It meets needs while protecting resources and considering long-term impacts."}]'::jsonb)
)
update public.curriculum_document_notes as n
set
  summary = r.summary,
  study_sections = r.study_sections,
  review_status = 'needs_review',
  publication_status = 'draft'
from revised_notes as r
where n.id = r.id;

